# IINA issue #5909 — "Scrub by Mouse Drag Mishandles Next Video"

## ROOT CAUSE

`PlayerCore.seek(percent:forceExact:)` at `iina/PlayerCore.swift:952-963` — the
defensive clamp `percent.clamped(to: 0..<100)` is effectively a no-op against
mpv's "seek‑to‑EOF advances the playlist" behavior, so each slider drag tick
fires a near-EOF seek that pushes mpv to the next file; because the slider's
mouse-position-driven tracking has no notion of "file identity," every
subsequent drag tick repeats the same near-EOF seek against whatever file is
now current, producing a cascade.

## CONTROL FLOW

1. User drags the play-slider knob past the visible right edge of the slider.
   AppKit's `NSSliderCell` clamps the slider's `doubleValue` to `maxValue` (set
   to `100` in `PlaySliderCell.awakeFromNib`,
   `iina/PlaySliderCell.swift:39-42`).

2. `MainWindowController.playSliderChanges(_:)`
   (`iina/MainWindowController.swift:3246`) fires for each drag tick and calls
   `super.playSliderChanges(sender)`.

3. `PlayerWindowController.playSliderChanges(_:)`
   (`iina/PlayerWindowController.swift:689-693`) computes
   `percentage = 100 * sender.doubleValue / sender.maxValue` (= 100 when the
   knob is pinned at the right edge) and calls
   `player.seek(percent: 100, forceExact: !followGlobalSeekTypeWhenAdjustSlider)`
   (default `forceExact: true`).

4. `PlayerCore.seek(percent:forceExact:)` (`iina/PlayerCore.swift:952-963`)
   applies `percent = percent.clamped(to: 0..<100)`. The half-open-range
   overload at `iina/Extensions.swift:413-420` returns
   `range.upperBound.nextDown` for any value `>= 100`, i.e.
   `100.0.nextDown ≈ 99.9999999999999986` — indistinguishable from EOF for
   mpv's purposes. The command issued is
   `seek 99.9999… absolute-percent+exact`.

5. mpv decodes precisely to that position; with `keep-open=yes` (the value
   produced for the default prefs `keepOpenOnFileEnd=true` and
   `playlistAutoPlayNext=true`, see
   `MPVController.mpvInit` at `iina/MPVController.swift:381-393`) it plays one
   frame past the seek target, hits end-of-file, fires `MPV_EVENT_END_FILE`,
   and advances to the next playlist entry.

6. A new file loads; `PlayerCore.fileLoaded()` (`iina/PlayerCore.swift:2087-…`)
   sets `info.videoDuration` to the new file's duration and the periodic
   `PlayerWindowController.updatePlayTime(...)`
   (`iina/PlayerWindowController.swift:606-635`) resets
   `playSlider.doubleValue` to the new file's
   `(pos / duration) * 100 ≈ 0`.

7. The user has not released the mouse, so `NSSliderCell` keeps tracking. On
   the next `mouseDragged` event, `continueTracking(...)` recomputes
   `doubleValue` from the cursor's pixel position — still pinned at the right
   edge — yielding `100` again. `PlaySliderCell.startTracking` /
   `stopTracking` (`iina/PlaySliderCell.swift:154-167`) only manage the
   pause-during-drag flag; nothing in the cell or in `playSliderChanges`
   binds the in-progress drag to the file it started on.

8. Steps 3-7 repeat each drag tick. Each tick = one near-EOF seek = one
   playlist advance. The user perceives "the player cascades through multiple
   files in the playlist as the user keeps dragging."

## WHY IT CASCADES

Two independent design choices combine to produce the cascade. First, the
"don't seek to EOF" guard in `seek(percent:)` is built on
`Double.clamped(to: 0..<100)`, but the half-open-range clamp returns
`100.0.nextDown`, which differs from `100.0` by a single ULP — far below any
threshold mpv uses to distinguish "near the end" from "at the end" when
performing an exact seek. The author was aware of this ("it still won't work
for videos with large keyframe interval"); in practice it doesn't even work
for normal keyframe intervals, because exact mode decodes to the requested
position, which is essentially the last sample, after which mpv's eof-on-end
behavior advances the playlist. Second, `NSSliderCell.continueTracking` is
mouse-position-driven and has no file identity: when the new file loads and
`updatePlayTime` resets `playSlider.doubleValue` to ~0, AppKit immediately
overwrites that value with the percentage implied by the current mouse pixel
on the next drag event (still ~100 because the cursor never left the right
edge). So each drag tick re-applies a near-100% seek to whatever file is
currently open in mpv, and the loop repeats until the user releases the mouse
button. The clamp is neither absolute-time-based nor drag-aware, so neither
the per-seek nor the per-drag boundary is enforced.

## SUGGESTED FIX

The most surgical fix lives in `PlayerCore.seek(percent:forceExact:)`: convert
the percent target to absolute seconds and clamp to `duration - ε` for an
`ε` large enough to keep mpv clear of its end-of-file decision (one second is
safely larger than any realistic keyframe interval for typical content), e.g.

```swift
if let duration = info.videoDuration?.second, duration > 0 {
  let maxPercent = max(0, (duration - 1.0) / duration * 100)
  percent = percent.clamped(to: 0...maxPercent)
}
```

This single change neutralizes the cascade entirely: the first slider tick
no longer triggers a playlist advance, and therefore no subsequent tick can
re-apply against a freshly-advanced file. A complementary, more semantically
honest fix is to capture `playerCore.info.currentURL` (or the playlist index)
in `PlaySliderCell.startTracking` and short-circuit `continueTracking` (or
`playSliderChanges`) when that identity changes mid-drag, so a slider drag is
unconditionally scoped to the file it began on; this is closer to the bug
report's framing ("clamped to the current one") and would also defeat any
other mechanism that could advance the file mid-drag.

DONE